1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,130
Sound familiar?

2
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:05,410
Three Linux machines on your
network. Three updates.

3
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:09,175
And the same packages are downloaded
from the Internet again and again.

4
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:14,417
What if your network had
its own package warehouse?

5
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:18,650
Download once. Store it locally.
Use it again and again.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,952
This is Cache & Carry.

7
00:00:23,052 --> 00:00:26,565
A lean, self-hosted package
cache for your network.

8
00:00:26,665 --> 00:00:32,514
The dashboard shows every request, every stored
package and how much bandwidth you save.

9
00:00:33,664 --> 00:00:36,549
On the first request, the
package comes from the mirror.

10
00:00:36,649 --> 00:00:38,151
After that, it is in the cache.

11
00:00:38,251 --> 00:00:41,172
The next machine gets it
straight from your network.

12
00:00:44,264 --> 00:00:51,360
And when several machines need the same file at the same
time, a single upstream download can serve them all.

13
00:00:51,460 --> 00:01:00,764
In our test network, an 8.6 MiB package index took
959 ms from the Internet. From the cache, 9 ms.

14
00:01:01,864 --> 00:01:05,678
Whether Proxmox, Debian,
Ubuntu, Arch or CachyOS:

15
00:01:05,728 --> 00:01:10,014
Cache & Carry brings your supported
package sources under one roof.

16
00:01:10,114 --> 00:01:14,202
As a lean systemd service, for
example in a Proxmox container.

17
00:01:15,152 --> 00:01:19,556
Cache & Carry is free on GitHub, open
source and ready for your homelab.

18
00:01:19,656 --> 00:01:25,621
If you try it, leave a star, share the project
and tell me in the comments how you use it.
